is not prompting; it is keeping the instructional decisions and delegating only the implementation. AIL 606 frames this as a division of labor: the designer owns what the interaction should be and why, and Codex owns turning that specification into working code, with every accepted and rejected suggestion documented.\n\nThis section teaches you to specify the pedagogy precisely enough that the AI implements your design rather than substituting its own. You should leave able to rewrite a vague \"make me an app\" prompt into an instructional specification, and able to recognize when generated code encodes a teaching mistake (rewarding wrong answers, fusing content with logic) that you must reject.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Specify the instructional interaction precisely enough that the tool implements your design, not its defaults.\n- Keep design decisions (sequence, feedback, separation of content) with the human author.\n- Audit generated code for encoded pedagogy errors, not just for whether it runs.\n- Document each accepted and rejected suggestion with the instructional reason.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student prompts Codex with \"Make me a good flashcard app for my module.\" The tool returns working code: all card data crammed into one global string with answers inline, and a confetti animation that fires on every click, including wrong answers. The code runs and looks done. But the prompt delegated the design (\"a good app\"), so the tool supplied its own defaults: a data structure no non-coder teammate can edit, answers exposed in the markup, and a reward that celebrates errors. Each of these is a teaching decision the student never consciously made.\n\nThe case shows that the most consequential moment in AI-assisted authoring is the prompt. A prompt that hands over the instructional decision gets generic pedagogy back; a prompt that specifies the interaction gets implementation back.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nThe governing principle is the division of cognitive labor: the human author holds the instructional decisions, and the generative tool holds the implementation. An instructional decision answers a teaching question, such as when feedback appears, whether the answer is hidden until the learner commits, how content is separated from logic so non-coders can edit it, and which behaviors are rewarded. An implementation detail answers a construction question, such as which loop or data structure realizes that decision. When you let the tool make instructional decisions, you inherit its defaults, which are tuned for a generic average and often violate basic pedagogy (rewarding any click, exposing answers).\n\nA precise specification is the instrument that enforces the division. Compare \"make a good flashcard app\" with \"read cards from a separate JSON array, flip on click, reveal the answer only after a flip, show brief feedback, and play no celebratory animation on incorrect responses.\" The second prompt has already made every instructional decision; Codex is left only with implementation. The separation of content from logic deserves special note, because it is both an architecture choice and a pedagogy choice: when content lives in a separate file, a non-coding subject expert can revise the cards, and answers are not leaked into the markup.\n\n| Owned by the human author (decisions) | Owned by the tool (implementation) |\n| --- | --- |\n| When feedback appears, and for which responses | The function that renders the feedback |\n| Whether the answer is hidden until a flip | The state variable tracking flip status |\n| That content lives separate from logic | The import statement and file format |\n| Which behaviors earn a reward | The animation library call |\n\nThe reason auditing matters as much as prompting is that even a good prompt can produce code that quietly encodes a mistake. \"Runs without errors\" is a construction test; \"rewards only correct answers, hides answers until commit, keeps content editable\" is the pedagogy test, and only the human can run it.\n\nSort the labor: match each decision to the side that must own it. The human owns the teaching question; the tool owns the construction question. Every decision you leave on the tool's side becomes a default you inherit.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"When feedback appears and for which responses\",\"definition\":\"Human author: a teaching decision\"},{\"term\":\"Whether the answer is hidden until a flip\",\"definition\":\"Human author: a teaching decision\"},{\"term\":\"That content lives in a file separate from logic\",\"definition\":\"Human author: a teaching decision\"},{\"term\":\"The state variable that tracks flip status\",\"definition\":\"Generative tool: an implementation detail\"},{\"term\":\"The function that renders the feedback text\",\"definition\":\"Generative tool: an implementation detail\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A prompt asks Codex to make a good flashcard app, and the returned code fires confetti on every click, including wrong answers. Whose decision was the reward-on-wrong-answers behavior, and why?\" explanation=\"The prompt delegated the design by asking for a good app, so the tool supplied its own default reward rule. The author never consciously chose to reward errors; that instructional decision was made by the tool because the prompt left it open.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The tool made it, because the prompt left the reward condition open instead of specifying it.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The author made it deliberately by choosing confetti.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"No one; confetti on every click is pedagogically neutral.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The tool made it, but that is fine because the code runs.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nWork through one annotated before/after revision of a Codex-assisted authoring decision for a flashcard widget.\n\n**Before (prompt and what Codex returned):**\n\nPrompt: \"Make me a good flashcard app for my module.\"\n\n```js\nlet cards = \"Q1:A1;Q2:A2;Q3:A3\"; // all data in one global string, answers inline in markup\nfunction showConfetti() { /* fires on every click, even wrong answers */ }\n```\n\n**Annotations a reviewer would leave:**\n\n1. The prompt handed the design decision (\"a good flashcard app\") to Codex instead of specifying the interaction.\n2. Content and logic are fused in one global string, so a non-coder on the team cannot edit cards and the answers are exposed in the markup.\n3. Confetti fires on every click, including wrong answers, which rewards errors and adds extraneous load.\n\n**After (revised prompt and accepted code):**\n\nPrompt: \"Generate a flashcard component that reads cards from a separate JSON array, flips on click, reveals the answer only after a flip, and shows brief feedback; no celebratory animation on incorrect responses. I will write the card content.\"\n\n```js\nimport cards from \"./cards.json\"; // content separated from logic, editable by non-coders\n// feedback text comes from the card object; positive animation gated to correct answers only\n```\n\n**Why the after version is stronger:** the designer kept the instructional decisions (separation of content and logic, answer hidden until flip, animation only on correct) and used Codex to implement them. Each accepted or rejected suggestion is documented with its reason, so a reviewer can audit why the confetti-on-every-click behavior was dropped. The change is a defensible authoring decision, not just code that runs.\n\nYou are authoring the flashcard widget with Codex. Walk the division of labor from prompt to audit, deciding what to keep with you and what to delegate.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Keeping the decisions, delegating the implementation\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"You are about to open Codex to build the deck. What do you write first?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"A specification that fixes feedback timing, answer reveal, content separation, and reward conditions\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"Right. The specification is both your prompt and your audit checklist.\"},{\"label\":\"A one-line goal: make me a good flashcard app for my module\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"This delegates the design; the tool will supply generic defaults you never examined.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"Codex returns code that reads cards from one inline global string with answers in the markup. The build runs. Accept?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Reject; content fused with logic means non-coders cannot edit and answers are exposed\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"Running is the floor, not the standard. Separation is a pedagogy decision you specified.\"},{\"label\":\"Accept, because it runs without errors\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Running is a construction test, not a pedagogy test.\"}]},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"In testing, the deck celebrates wrong answers and a teammate cannot edit the cards. What does this reveal about the workflow?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Leaving instructional decisions open let the tool encode pedagogy you did not choose\",\"next\":\"n1\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly; go back and specify the decisions before prompting.\"}],\"feedback\":\"You inherited the defaults the tool supplied.\"},\"n4\":{\"prompt\":\"Codex also offers a confetti animation that fires on every click. How do you handle it in your record?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Reject it and document the reason: rewarding wrong answers contradicts the formative purpose\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"Accept-or-reject-with-reason is what makes the authoring auditable and feeds the disclosure.\"},{\"label\":\"Accept it silently because animations look polished\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"Undocumented acceptance of a pedagogy mistake is exactly what the audit exists to catch.\"}]},\"end\":{\"prompt\":\"The prompt fixed every instructional decision, the code passed the pedagogy audit, and each accepted or rejected suggestion carries its reason.\",\"feedback\":\"You owned the decisions and delegated only the implementation.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nDecide the pedagogy before you prompt, then audit the output as pedagogy. For each AI-assisted authoring step:\n\n1. **Specification check:** Does the prompt state every instructional decision (feedback timing, answer reveal, content separation, reward conditions) so only implementation is left to the tool?\n2. **Encoded-pedagogy check:** Does the generated code encode any teaching mistake (rewarding errors, exposing answers, fusing content with logic)?\n3. **Documentation check:** Is each accepted and rejected suggestion recorded with its instructional reason?\n\nIf the prompt left an instructional decision open, the tool made it for you, and you now own a decision you never examined.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first error is treating prompting as the skill, when the real skill is deciding the pedagogy before prompting. The second is accepting code because it runs; running is necessary but says nothing about whether the interaction teaches. The third is treating AI assistance as authorship that needs no disclosure, when in fact every generated piece needs an audit and a record of what you kept and changed.\n\nThe author's job is to own the instructional decisions and verify the implementation, not to outsource judgment to whatever the tool returns.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nWrite the specification before opening the tool. List every instructional decision the component embodies: feedback timing, answer reveal, content-logic separation, reward conditions. This list is your prompt and also your audit checklist, which is why writing it first does double duty.\n\nThen prompt with the specification, not the goal. \"Implement this interaction\" gets you implementation; \"make something good\" gets you the tool's defaults. The more decisions your prompt fixes, the less room the tool has to substitute its own pedagogy.\n\nFinally, audit against the same list and record the result. Run the pedagogy test (does it reward only correct answers, hide answers until commit, keep content editable), not just the build. For each suggestion the tool offered, note whether you accepted or rejected it and why, because that record is what makes your authoring auditable and feeds directly into the disclosure artifact.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: **claim** on the left, **evidence** on the right. The claim is the instructional decision you intended; the evidence is the line of the prompt that specified it and the line of generated code that implemented it. By the end of AIL 606 04.06, your notes should let a reviewer see which decisions stayed with you and which were delegated.\n\nA strong reader of AI-assisted authoring can answer four questions:\n\n1. Which instructional decisions did the prompt fix, and which did it leave open?\n2. Does the generated code encode any pedagogy the author did not intend?\n3. Which suggestion was rejected, and for what instructional reason?\n4. How would the specification change for a different learner or interaction?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is strong when the prompt fixes every instructional decision, the generated code passes the pedagogy audit, and each accepted or rejected suggestion is recorded with its reason. Treat \"it runs\" as the floor, not the standard. Where you are unsure whether the tool made a decision you should have made, mark it with the annotation tools and ask BigAL to compare a goal-level prompt with a specification-level prompt rather than for general prompting tips.\n\nA useful final paragraph for the artifact would sound like this: \"I specified the flip-then-reveal interaction, separate content file, and reward-only-on-correct rule, and used Codex to implement them. I rejected its confetti-on-every-click default, because rewarding wrong answers contradicts the formative purpose of the deck.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same workflow from a flashcard widget to an auto-graded short-answer question. The implementation Codex can supply changes, but the division of labor does not: you still own the instructional decisions, and they get harder. Now you must specify how strict the matching is (case, whitespace, synonyms), what counts as partial credit, and what feedback a near-miss receives, because a generative tool will default to brittle exact-match grading that fails \"Paris\" against \"paris.\" Transfer means writing a richer specification for a higher-stakes interaction, not reusing the flashcard prompt. If you understand the division of labor, a higher-stakes interaction visibly demands more decisions from you, not from the tool.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/04-06-ai-assisted-authoring-with-codex-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"04.06\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes for AIL 606 04.06. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_ai_assisted_authoring_with_codex\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_ai_assisted_authoring_with_codex\", \"ail606_supplement_design_draft_and_theory_to_prototype_alignment\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm that your prompt fixed every instructional decision, your audit checked the code as pedagogy rather than as a build, and each suggestion you kept or dropped carries its reason. That record is what makes AI-assisted authoring defensible.\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_4_6_ai_assisted_authoring_with_codex_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_ai_assisted_authoring_with_codex", "stem": "You prompt Codex to scaffold an interactive flashcard widget for your prototype. It generates working code that stores all card data in a single global variable and hardcodes the answers in the HTML. As an instructional designer, what is the first thing to evaluate?", "options": ["Whether the code runs without errors, since that is the only thing that matters.", "Whether the generated structure supports the learning interaction you designed, such as separating content from logic so non-coders can edit cards and so answers are not exposed to the learner.", "Whether the variable names match a style guide.", "Whether Codex used the newest JavaScript syntax."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Codex optimizes for code that runs, not for instructional or maintainability requirements. The designer evaluates whether the structure serves the intended learning interaction and authoring workflow.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_4_6_codex_runs_means_correct", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_ai_assisted_authoring_with_codex"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_4_6_ai_assisted_authoring_with_codex_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_ai_assisted_authoring_with_codex", "stem": "Which prompt to Codex reflects sound design-first authoring rather than handing over the design decision?", "options": ["\"Make me a good quiz for my module.\"", "\"Generate a 5-item single-select quiz component that shows per-item feedback immediately, keyboard-navigable, with answers stored separately from the markup; I will write the items and feedback text.\"", "\"Build whatever quiz you think works best and pick the questions too.\"", "\"Write a quiz and make it look impressive.\""], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "The strong prompt specifies the learning interaction and constraints the designer already decided, leaving Codex to implement, not to make the pedagogical choices.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_4_6_codex_design_handover", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_ai_assisted_authoring_with_codex"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_4_6_ai_assisted_authoring_with_codex_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ai_supported_learning", "stem": "Codex suggests adding an animated confetti burst every time a learner answers, including on wrong answers. What is the strongest bounded response?", "options": ["Keep it, because positive feedback is always motivating.", "Reject it for wrong answers and reconsider its frequency, since celebrating errors and constant animation can mislead learners and add extraneous load; accept the suggestion only where it supports the learning goal.", "Remove all feedback animations to be safe.", "Ask Codex whether the confetti is pedagogically sound and follow its answer."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "An AI authoring suggestion is a proposal to judge against learning goals. Confetti on wrong answers conflicts with accurate feedback, so the designer accepts it selectively or rejects it.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_4_6_codex_suggestion_as_requirement", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_4_6_ai_assisted_authoring_with_codex_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "evidence_based_revision", "stem": "After Codex generates your prototype's scoring logic, you notice it marks an answer correct only if the casing matches exactly. Which revision note best documents an evidence-based fix?", "options": ["\"Fixed the bug.\"", "\"Codex used a case-sensitive string match; I changed it to normalize case and trim whitespace because lear